WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.960
Here are the five rules of free speech and corporations.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.580
Rule one. Corporations have First Amendment rights.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.160
The Supreme Court's first decision protecting individual's free expression rights

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.700
came in 1931.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:18.060
It's first decision protecting a corporation's free expression rights came just five years later in 1936.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.240
That decision involved a newspaper corporation.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.520
But the courts first decision protecting a non-media business corporation's free expression rights

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.620
came five years after that in 1941.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.260
From the 1950's onward, many court decisions protected for-profit corporations.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.120
Indeed the very first American court decision striking down a state's statute on free speech grounds

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.220
took place in 1894. And it protected the rights of a corporation.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.820
When the Supreme Court split sharply over corporate speech in the 2010 Citizens United case,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.760
no one doubted that the First Amendment protects corporations generally.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.260
The question was whether there was an exception for corporate speech supporting or opposing

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.480
political candidates.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.480
Why is this so?

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.540
Partly because corporations partly are, after all, made up of people.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.360
If the government takes a corporations property, that doesn't hurt the corporation in some abstract sense,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.600
it hurts the corporation's stockholders.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.180
If the government stops the New York Times company from criticizing the president,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:16.680
that restricts the First Amendment rights of the newspaper's editors.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.040
Rule two. The media doesn't have any greater First Amendment rights than other speakers.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.720
The freedom of the press isn't the freedom of a business category called "the press."

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.480
It has been understood since the 1700's as the freedom of all to use the printing press

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.060
and its technological heirs.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.760
There are some statutes that give institutional media special additional rights

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.840
beyond what the First Amendment gives them.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.100
But the Constitution doesn't distinguish reporters from bloggers, or media businesses from other businesses.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.140
This means that the First Amendment protects General Motors and Walmart

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.660
as much as it protects the New York Times or CNN or the New Republic.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.760
If GM's corporate speech could be restricted, then the New York Time's speech could be too.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.540
And because the New York Times' speech can't be restricted, neither can GM's.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.920
This, by the way, means that First Amendment law doesn't have to decide who is media and who isn't.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.909
Is Google media?

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:06.960
How about Amazon?

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.900
Which sells electronics, sells books, and makes movies.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.440
The Supreme Court doesn't have to decide because all corporations have First Amendment rights

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.460
regardless of whether they are media.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.500
Rule three. Union's have free speech rights too.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.800
Citizens United struck down the federal law that banned both corporations and unions

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.940
from speaking out for or against political candidates.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.520
Rule four. Individual stockholders can't veto corporations political spending,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.800
whether those corporations publish newspapers or make widgets.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.440
Generally speaking, American corporations are run on majority of shared rules basis.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.000
Individual objectors can generally sell their stock, but they can't order managers around.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.160
That's a familiar rule for all sorts of spending.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.100
If you don't like Ben and Jerry's liberal messages, you don't have to buy their ice cream.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.900
But you can't just buy a share and then demand that they stop saying things

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.860
that you, as a minority stockholder, dislike.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.040
Likewise, if you don't like a company's charitable contributions,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.520
or the tone of it's advertising, or it's speech opposing unionization,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.000
you can't stop such corporate action even if you own shares.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.780
And you can't control the New York Times editorial policy even if you are a stockholder.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.340
The same is true for spending about policital candidates.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.780
If a corporation wants to endorse a candidate,

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.620
dissenting shareholders can't stop that

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.260
anymore than they can stop any of the corporations other action or speech.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:28.740
Rule five. Corporate and union direct contributions to candidate campaigns can be sharply limited,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.620
though independent spending is fully protected.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.380
The rationale for this is complicated, but basically direct contributions of money to candidates,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.380
whether by corporations or individuals,

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.960
are less constitutionally protected than speech.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.340
Including expensive speech by those corporations or individuals.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.160
So to sum up.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.380
Restrictions on corporate speech generally violate the First Amendment

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.680
to the same extent that restrictions on individual speech do.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.000
Some justices think there should be an exception for speech supporting or opposing candidates,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.760
and maybe ballot measures, but all the justices agree

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.600
that the general rule is that corporations have free speech rights.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.500
The institutional media get no special First Amendment rights beyond what others have.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.260
So if corporate speech could be restricted,

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.760
speech by corporate-owned newspapers, magazines, book publishers,

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.840
movie studios, and the like, could be restricted too.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.720
Union speech is constitutionally protected also.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.360
The objecting minority shareholders can't block corporate speech with which they disagree.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.320
Just as they can't block corporate charitable contributions, advertising campaigns,

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.740
or product designs with which they disagree.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.140
Corporate and union direct contributions to candidate campaigns can be sharply limited.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.760
Though independent spending is fully protected.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.300
I'm Eugene Volokh and I approve this message.

